# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Maximum of two numbers".
id: maximum
title: Maximum of two numbers
topic: Conditional
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Maximum of two numbers"
tags: conditional, wp, consequence

task:
  The program stores in $m$ the larger of $x$ and $y$. Calculate the
  weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition $Q$: first
  the weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the
  conditional. Then use the consequence rule to decide whether the triple
  is valid.

given:
  Condition B: $x >= y$

pre: true
post: m >= x && m >= y && (m = x || m = y)

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if x >= y then {
    {R1}
    m := x           // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    m := y           // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch m := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($m$) with the assignment
  expression ($x$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(m := x, Q)
  = (m >= x && m >= y && (m = x || m = y))[m <- x]
  = x >= x && x >= y && (x = x || x = y)    // every m is replaced by x
  = true && x >= y && (x = x || x = y)      // x >= x holds for every x
  = true && x >= y && true                  // x = x is true, and true || A is true
  = x >= y                                  // true && A is A, and A && true is A
set: R1 = x >= y

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch m := y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($m$) with the assignment
  expression ($y$) in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(m := y, Q)
  = (m >= x && m >= y && (m = x || m = y))[m <- y]
  = y >= x && y >= y && (y = x || y = y)    // every m is replaced by y
  = y >= x && true && (y = x || y = y)      // y >= y holds for every y
  = y >= x && true && true                  // y = y is true, and A || true is true
  = y >= x                                  // A && true is A
set: R2 = y >= x

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x >= y$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x >= y : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x >= y) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if x >= y then m := x else m := y, Q)
  = (x >= y ==> x >= y) && (!(x >= y) ==> y >= x)   // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = true && (!(x >= y) ==> y >= x)                  // A ==> A is true
  = !(x >= y) ==> y >= x                            // true && A is A
  = x < y ==> y >= x                                // !(x >= y) is x < y
  = true                                            // x < y implies y >= x
set: R3 = true

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: true ==> true
why:
  $R3$ is $true$, and every formula implies $true$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  In each branch, the assignment makes $m$ equal to $x$ or to $y$, so the
  part $m = x || m = y$ of $Q$ always holds. What remains is the condition
  of the branch, which is known to hold there. So the program works from
  every initial state.
